{
  "id": "pf-household-command-center",
  "name": "The Alvarez family command center — six years of bolted-on household databases",
  "family": "personal-family",
  "inspiration": "notion.com/templates — the 'Family Command Center' / household-planner family (chores + meal planning + bills + reading lists), crossed with the naming habits of the real 37-database workspace dump: a '(SB)'-suffixed database, a 'Meal Calendar DB'-style decorated type word, per-person duplicated lists in the manner of 'Notebooks (SB)' / 'All notes (SB)', and 'Status' recurring across siblings with mutually incompatible option lists.",
  "notes": "A family of five has run the whole household out of one workspace for six years. Nothing was designed as a system: every database was added the week it was needed, by whichever family member needed it, so naming drifts ('🧹 Weekly Chores', 'Recipe Box (SB)', 'Meal Plan DB', 'Pet Care Log ' with a real trailing space, 'Priority (v2)', a lowercase 'account'), and three separate personal reading lists were never consolidated.\n\nDecision points exercised: (1) exact duplicates — Mia's and Leo's reading lists are byte-identical; (2) same concept, disjoint schemas — 🧹 Weekly Chores has 2 columns and Household Projects has 12, both 'stuff to get done around the house', and merging them would hang Contractor Phone and Permit Required off every dishwashing row; (3) a four-way 'Status' split with mutually incompatible vocabularies; (4) identical-option 'Genre' across the three reading lists and identical-option 'Assigned To' across chores and projects, where the projects copy carries a trailing space in its NAME; (5) 'Assigned To' as multiSelect in chores but free text in the pet log; (6) all six legal bundled targets land in this one fixture; (7) eight date/checkbox traps that must stay themselves; (9) scale variety, 2 properties up to 12; (10) messy real-world naming throughout.\n\nThe near-duplicate is the point of the reading lists: Mom's list is Mia's five columns plus 'Format' and 'Borrowed From', so each member covers 0.71 / 0.71 / 1.00 of the seven-property union. A planner that demands an exact column match will refuse the merge and leave the family with three interchangeable book types.\n\nDeliberately NOT asserted, because a thoughtful product person could argue either way:\n  - Household Projects 'Contractor Phone' / 'Contractor Email' are phone- and email-format, so the whitelist would happily grab them, but they are the CONTRACTOR's contact details hanging off a renovation page, not the page's own. Whether a phone-format property on a non-person page should still become the bundled Phone relation is a real design question; the fixture leaves it open and pins only the unambiguous case on Family Contacts.\n  - 'Status' in the three reading lists is also identical in name, format and options and would naturally share one relation, exactly like 'Genre' does. It is left out so the shared-relation assertions stay minimal and the four-way Status split cannot be confused with it.\n  - 'Store' is a curated select in Grocery List (Mercadona / Costco / corner shop) and free text in the warranty tracker; the format difference makes sharing impossible, but only the 'Assigned To' format clash is asserted, as the semantically interesting one.\n  - Family Calendar 'Who' carries the same five family-member options as 'Assigned To' under a different name. Whether a planner should unify a synonym pair is taste, so it is not asserted either way.\n  - Meal Plan DB 'Date', Pet Care Log 'Date' and Family Calendar 'Date' are three plain dates with no options; nothing here says whether they should be one relation or three.",
